speechtext:
I will give the date shortly. The regulations give effect to the policy of the parole board which has been evolved after lengthy discussions between the chairman of the board, myself, officers of the penitentiary branch and of the department as to the general principles which govern the board in granting parole. I should like to emphasize to this committee that these regulations give effect to the policy of the board which has been settled, amongst which are the following basic principles:
That the policy of the board is as far as possible to encourage inmates to become law abiding citizens and to assist them to do so by granting parole;
To treat the offender rather than the offence;
To deal with offenders as individuals not as members of a group;
To judge each case objectively according to its merits and circumstances;
To avoid the use of rigid arbitrary rules of practice;
To be practical, realistic and businesslike in dealing with offenders and to avoid any suggestion that parole involves mollycoddling of the inmates or the use of leniency or clemency;
To consider each case from the point of view of what the inmate is apt to do in the future as more significant than what he has done in the past and to provide adequate supervision;
To ensure protection of the public and assistance for parolees; and finally,
To emphasize correction and reform as the purposes of punishment rather than vengeance or retribution.
Within the ambit of that policy and under the authority of the regulations the board approaches each case on the basis of the attitude

of the inmate and the likelihood of his profiting by his parole, and his ambition and intention to re-enter society as a law abiding and useful member thereof rather than giving effect to any lenient or clement or special circumstance which the inmate puts up on his own behalf.
As I said earlier, the case of every inmate is automatically reviewed within six months of his admission, and from that time on it comes up for further review within a year and every two years thereafter. But the policy of the board is that the qualifications of an inmate to be granted parole are looked at on the basis of his attitude and the likelihood of his living as a law abiding member of society rather than on any arbitrary assessment or report that may be made about him.
